{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Data Warehouse vs Operational Database "}, {"type": "position", "content": 11}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 3}, {"type": "level", "content": "B1.1"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use to talk about operational and warehouse databases."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Designs the data structure based on the database structures and its characteristics.  Understands the concept of data warehouse, its components and characteristics."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Discusses a range of options for potential actions at work through the correct use of grammar, vocabulary; level of formality and purpose, and cohesion and coherence aspects appropriate to this level.  Exchanges simple information on the data validation and data warehouses."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe8n5le03ob11re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Present perfect vs simple past: negative statements"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<p>You can use the <strong>present perfect</strong> and <strong>simple past</strong> at the same time to contrast information between what you did in the past (<strong>simple past) </strong>and what you started in the past and continue doing in the present (<strong>present&nbsp;</strong><strong>perfect</strong>).</p>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p>To create a negative statement using <strong>simple past</strong>, you need to use the auxiliary a verb DID and NOT within its base form.</p> <p>Structure:</p> <p>Pronoun + Aux + Not + Verb + Complement</p> <p>Example:<i> I <strong>did not know</strong> the differences between operational databases and data warehouse.</i></p> <p>To create a negative statement using <strong><i>present perfect</i></strong>, you need to use the auxiliaries HAVE (I-you-we-they) and HAS (he-she-it) and NOT with a verb in past participle form.</p> <p>Structure:</p> <p>Pronoun + Aux + Not + Verb (past participle) + Complement</p> <p>Example: <i>Operational databases <strong>haven&rsquo;t</strong><strong>been</strong><strong>developed</strong> online.</i></p> <p>You can include a <strong><i>simple past</i></strong> statement with a <strong><i>present perfect</i></strong> statement in one sentence to contrast actions that are completed and actions that have not been completed.</p> <p>Example:</p> <p><i>I didn't have all of the questions right, so I haven't gotten a good grade.</i></p>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "I didn't have those notes on my notebook; so I think I haven't paid attention during classes."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "good for you "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "indicates approval for something another person does  "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(expression)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Good for you! I didn't have all of the questions right, so I haven't gotten a good grade."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n5le03ob11aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "particular "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "that is unique "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: In other words, operational databases are ideal to look for particular information, since it maintains current data which might be important for current processes."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n5le03ob11aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "really "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "exclamation to exalt something "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(interjection)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I did really well. I got all of the answers right."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n5le03ob11aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "I mean"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "indicates that you want to say something in a different way"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(expression)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: On the other hand, data warehouse displays data in a general way, I mean, it maintains data in a historical way. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n5le03ob11aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "transaction "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a type of interaction between two parties"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Look, operational databases haven't been developed online, but they have been developed in transaction processing while data warehouse has focused on online analytical processing."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n5le03ob11aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "ideal "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "that is the best "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: In other words, operational databases are ideal to look for particular information, since it maintains current data which might be important for current processes.Example: In other words, operational databases are ideal to look for particular information, since it maintains current data which might be important for current processes."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n5le03ob11aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Find examples about data warehouse and operational databases."}, {"type": "text", "content": "In a poster, write the most important examples combining words and pictures. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Give an oral presentation to explain the examples written in the poster."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I find information about data warehouse and operational databases."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I write in a poster the most important examples. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I give an oral presentation to explain the most important examples of data warehouse and operational databases."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Operational database vs data warehouse"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<p>There are some different characteristics for <strong>operational</strong> <strong>databases</strong> and <strong>data</strong> <strong>warehouse</strong>.</p>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p>Something really important for companies is to handle databases correctly. Data must be available at any time, that&rsquo;s why there are two types of databases; operational and warehouse.</p> <p><strong>Data</strong> <strong>warehouse</strong> contains all of the data stored from an organization; it basically aims for analyzing information and answer some simple questions as who or what. On the other hand, <strong>operational</strong> <strong>databases</strong> are refreshed regularly and contains current information.</p> <p>The main characteristic of these databases is that both have the same goal, which is to save and organize data either in a historical way or an updatable way. </p>"}], "type": "learning_points"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "type": "welcome", "id": 0, "contents": [{}]}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "100", "image": "enpe8n5le03ob11re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<p><strong>Walter</strong>: Laura, how did you do in the test?</p>\n<p><strong>Laura:</strong> I did really well. I got all of the answers right.</p>\n<p><strong>Walter</strong>: Good for you! I didn&rsquo;t have all of the questions right, so I haven&rsquo;t gotten a good grade.</p>\n<p><strong>Laura:</strong> What questions did you get wrong?</p>\n<p><strong>Walter:</strong> Well, one of the questions is why data warehouse is different from operational databases?</p>"}, {"time": "19000", "image": "enpe8n5le03ob11re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<p><strong>Laura:</strong> That&rsquo;s an easy question. Look, operational databases haven&rsquo;t been developed online, but they have been developed in transaction processing while data warehouse has focused on online analytical processing. In other words, operational databases are ideal to look for particular information since it maintains current data which might be important for current processes. On the other hand, data warehouse displays data in a general way, I mean, it maintains data in a historical way.</p>\n<p><strong>Walter:</strong> I got it! I didn&rsquo;t have those notes on my notebook; so I think I haven&rsquo;t paid attention during classes.</p>\n<p><strong>Laura</strong>: What&rsquo;s the other question?</p>"}, {"time": "57200", "image": "enpe8n5le03ob11re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<p><strong>Walter:</strong> It says; can you give and explain examples of both data warehouse and operational databases?</p>\n<p><strong>Laura:</strong> This is a little bit more complex, but it&rsquo;s not that difficult. Remember that an operational database is usually process-oriented or process-driven, I mean, it concentrates on particular business processes such as registration or billing.</p>\n<p><strong>Walter:</strong> What about data warehouse?</p>\n<p><strong>Laura:</strong> Data in a warehouse is non-volatile. It means that new data can be incorporated any time is needed without changing any old information. That&rsquo;s why I told you that data warehouse keeps data in a historical way. Whenever you need any information it will be available to only be read.</p>"}], "coverimg": "enpe8n5le03ob11re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe8n5le03ob11re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 1, 